WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.125
Architecture centers on decisions that are
difficult to change and strongly affect a

00:00:05.125 --> 00:00:10.677
system’s quality attributes. A component diagram
helps describe a system, but architectural

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:16.107
reasoning also explains why its boundaries,
dependencies, storage and recovery mechanisms

00:00:16.107 --> 00:00:16.839
were chosen.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.445
Whether a choice is architectural depends on
context. A language choice may deeply affect

00:00:22.445 --> 00:00:27.952
latency, maintainability or hiring for a long-
lived system; for a disposable prototype its

00:00:27.952 --> 00:00:33.519
consequences may be smaller. Avoid declaring a
technology universally architectural without

00:00:33.519 --> 00:00:36.701
identifying the quality impact and cost of
reversal.

00:00:36.701 --> 00:00:42.189
For a new campus alerting service, ask which
decisions determine whether acknowledged alerts

00:00:42.189 --> 00:00:47.259
survive a crash, whether operators can diagnose
a failure, and whether a new delivery

00:00:47.259 --> 00:00:52.509
channel can be added. These questions connect
the opening lecture to later requirements,

00:00:52.509 --> 00:00:54.835
database, microservices and QODA units.

00:00:54.835 --> 00:00:59.579
Is a language choice always an architectural
decision? Its importance depends on how

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:04.549
strongly it affects relevant qualities and how
costly it is to reverse in the particular

00:01:04.549 --> 00:01:04.945
system.

00:01:04.945 --> 00:01:10.207
The example’s simplified observability pipeline
ingests telemetry, persists it, processes

00:01:10.207 --> 00:01:15.528
and indexes it, and supports alerts and queries.
Those functions depend on one another: an

00:01:15.528 --> 00:01:19.548
alert cannot be trustworthy if the relevant data
was never ingested.

00:01:19.548 --> 00:01:24.829
In the historical Datadog example, durability is
central to customer trust. Availability

00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:30.169
priorities include preserving ingestion and
monitoring before less urgent query behavior.

00:01:30.169 --> 00:01:35.450
Another important measure is ingestion-to-use
latency, including the time until data can

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:40.730
support an alert. This ordering belongs to that
business context, not every application.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:45.708
A quality requirement should state which user
outcome it protects. For a campus safety

00:01:45.708 --> 00:01:50.512
system, preserving an accepted urgent alert and
delivering it on time might outrank

00:01:50.512 --> 00:01:55.779
historical report generation. State what may be
delayed, what may be dropped, and what must

00:01:55.779 --> 00:02:00.756
not be lost. Apply authorization and data-
retention constraints as part of the design.

00:02:00.756 --> 00:02:06.171
If a dashboard is unavailable but accepted data
is durably retained, are availability and

00:02:06.171 --> 00:02:11.463
durability identical outcomes? No. The service
can retain data while failing to provide

00:02:11.463 --> 00:02:16.512
timely access or alerts. Measure both properties
and explain the user consequences.

00:02:16.756 --> 00:02:22.348
The case describes the March 2023 Datadog outage
as a historical case study. An unattended

00:02:22.348 --> 00:02:28.064
operating-system update restarted networking
software and disrupted routing on many nodes. A

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:33.346
change outside the ordinary staged rollout path
crossed otherwise separate deployment

00:02:33.346 --> 00:02:34.029
boundaries.

00:02:34.256 --> 00:02:39.797
The lesson is about correlated causes. Multiple
machines, regions or cloud providers do not

00:02:39.797 --> 00:02:45.094
guarantee independence when a shared update
mechanism can affect them together. Include

00:02:45.094 --> 00:02:50.513
operating-system packages, configuration,
identity, networking and management services in

00:02:50.513 --> 00:02:55.749
the dependency and change-control review.
Recovery behavior also differed across cloud

00:02:55.749 --> 00:02:57.880
environments in the case described.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:03.034
Replacing a machine and rebooting a machine can
have different consequences for local state.

00:03:03.034 --> 00:03:07.909
Treat these as historical observations from the
recording, not guarantees about current

00:03:07.909 --> 00:03:12.784
cloud-provider behavior. A recovery plan must
specify what survives each action and how

00:03:12.784 --> 00:03:14.521
service state is reconstructed.

00:03:14.521 --> 00:03:20.391
Can a geographically replicated system still
have one broad failure mechanism? Yes. A shared

00:03:20.391 --> 00:03:25.879
configuration, update, credential or control-
plane dependency can fail across regions.

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:31.302
Geographic diversity protects only against the
failure classes it actually separates.
